  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nSurface analysis shows high pressure centered over the lower Great \nLakes Region, which has resulted in a brief period of seasonally \ncool weather. Temperatures early this afternoon are sitting in the \nmiddle 70s with dewpoints in the low 50s. Similar conditions are \nexpected on Friday, though southerly return flow will advect \nmoisture back northward later in the day as the surface high pushes \noff to our east. \n\nAn upper shortwave will track from the Central Plains into the Ohio \nValley region through Friday night, bringing increased cloudiness \nbeginning tonight. Scattered chances for rain return late Friday \nmorning into Friday night as the shortwave works across southwest \nparts of the area. Activity will be working against somewhat drier \nair initially, likely resulting in virga or sprinkles at the onset \nwith total QPF looking to be on the order of a few tenths of an \ninch. Light rain chances may linger into Saturday morning as a weak \nshortwave glides along a warm/stationary front positioned just to \nour south.\n\nUpper ridging becomes amplified over the western US by the weekend, \nresulting in mid-level height rises here locally. Temperatures will \nwarm back into the 80s on Saturday then surge into the 90s by \nSunday. Similar temperatures will stick around through Tuesday for \nsome with the Extreme Forecast Index showing values of 0.9-1, \nindicating that there is high confidence in an anomalous event.\nThis paired with dewpoints in the 70s will lead to heat indices\npeaking between 100 and 110 degrees each afternoon. Heat \nheadlines will likely be coordinated in the coming days.\n\nOutside of the heat, isolated chances for severe storms exist late \nSunday into Monday night as several shortwave impulses glide \nalong the eastern periphery of a strong upper ridge. A highly \nunstable and moderately sheared airmass will be in place ahead \nof the upper waves. If storms manage to initiate, severe hazards\nand heavy rainfall will be likely. However, forecast certainty \nremains low until mesoscale features become clearer.\n\nTemperatures will cool back to near typical summertime values behind \nof cold front by the middle of next week. The CPC 8-14 day outlook \nfavors near to slightly above normal temperatures and below normal \nprecipitation for the end of July/beginning of August.",
